# Template for apps/web/.env, which Next.js reads when the web app starts, is built or is
# type-checked (`pnpm dev`, `pnpm build`, `pnpm start`, `pnpm typecheck`). Copy it when the app
# has settings to change, and make the copy readable by your user only, because server settings
# can include secrets:
#
#   cp apps/web/.env.example apps/web/.env && chmod 600 apps/web/.env
#
# .env files are gitignored; never commit one. Next.js also reads .env.local, .env.development,
# .env.production and their .local variants from this folder, and variables already set in the
# shell, even to an empty value, win over every file. Next.js expands $NAME in values, so write \$
# for a literal dollar sign, and a # ends a value unless the value is in quotes.
# If a variable is missing or invalid, Next.js stops and names it (it never prints values):
# `pnpm dev` checks every variable, `pnpm build` the NEXT_PUBLIC_ ones that it copies into the
# JavaScript, and `pnpm start` the server ones. `pnpm typecheck` checks the NEXT_PUBLIC_ ones too
# (its `next typegen` loads the config as `next build` does), so a required one must also be set
# wherever the type check runs, the pre-commit hook and CI included.
#
# The web app reads no variables of its own yet, so this file has no entries. When it needs one,
# declare it in apps/web/src/env/ and document it here, following these rules:
#
# - Settings only the server may know (secrets, internal URLs) have plain names and go in
#   server-schema.ts. Code reads them through server.ts, which a Client Component cannot import.
# - Settings the browser needs have names that start with NEXT_PUBLIC_ and go in
#   public-schema.ts. When the app is built, Next.js copies their values into the JavaScript sent
#   to every visitor, so they are public: never put a secret in one, and rebuild after changing
#   one.
#
# WEB_HOST and WEB_PORT (where the server listens; 127.0.0.1 and 3000 by default) have no effect
# in this file: the package scripts pass them to Next.js, which opens the port before it reads
# .env files. Set them in the shell instead, for example: WEB_PORT=3010 pnpm dev
